I’ll begin by inventorying the measured pattern, metadata, and reusable analysis tools, then establish a constrained baseline before testing phase candidates. I’ll keep the phase chemistry and peak-shape evidence tied to the saved/reopened GSAS-II project throughout.

The context points to the Ca–W–O system fired at 850 °C, but I’m treating CaWO₄ as only the first hypothesis. I’m now extracting the independent peak inventory—including weak unmatched peaks—so residual CaO/WO₃ or non-1:1 calcium tungstates are not hidden by the dominant phase.

The measured inventory supports two strong assignments already: scheelite CaWO₄ and orthorhombic WO₃. The WO₃ triplet at 22.94/23.42/24.23° rules out the common monoclinic record under a shared angular model. Several residual families remain—including a real ~19.29° peak—so I’m keeping CaO, Ca(OH)₂, alternate Ca–W–O stoichiometries, and the monoclinic CaWO₄ record on the watch list while refining the two-phase baseline.

The first saved/replayed two-phase baseline converged reproducibly at Rwp = 13.61% (Rexp = 2.25%), so it is decisively incomplete as a final model. I’m now branching that exact project to test smooth-background dimensionality, polarization, and reflection-surface roughness before attributing the large intensity residuals to texture or adding phases.

The nuisance checks show that extra background terms barely move Rwp, while a one-parameter surface-roughness correction and plausible polarization do help. Texture is clearly supported for CaWO₄: March–Dollase [110] lowers Rwp from 12.91% to 11.54%, outperforming axial [001] (11.67%) and diagonal/other alternatives (12.06–12.74%). I’m retaining [110] provisionally and now testing whether WO₃ intensity mismatch is texture-related before revisiting the unmatched phase inventory.

The residual inventory has now identified the missing chemistry cleanly: a Ca₃WO₆ phase in the monoclinic Ca₃OsO₆ structure type. Its independent peak family maps at ~18.7/19.29, 22.02, 30.72/31.42/32.16, 36.60/37.54, 45.14, 55.98, and 57.02°, including the previously unexplained low-angle peaks. I’m generating the charge-balanced W-for-Os analogue, then will refine it and re-challenge CaO/Ca(OH)₂ as weak phases.

The Ca₃WO₆ analogue drops Rwp to ~8.2% and carries about 12 wt%, with chemically sensible W–O (~1.93 Å) and Ca–O (>2.25 Å) distances inherited from the isostructural Ca₃OsO₆ parent. The remaining dominant residuals are the WO₃ triplet/intensity family, so I’m checking the monoclinic WO₃ polymorph under the same background, geometry, profile, roughness, polarization, and texture model rather than treating the current record’s intensity mismatch as a chemistry failure.

The physical alternate wins decisively: monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c) reaches Rwp = 5.33% under the same accepted nuisance model, versus ~8.2% for orthorhombic WO₃. This also resolves the three low-angle WO₃ peaks without invoking a second oxide polymorph. I’m finishing the texture-axis comparison for monoclinic WO₃, then I’ll test phase-specific size versus strain and the weak CaO/Ca(OH)₂ watch list from this saved baseline.

The monoclinic model’s best WO₃ texture correction is small but supported ([010], MD ≈ 0.973 versus 1.000; Rwp 5.311%). The six matched size/strain branches are still converging from that exact saved baseline; once complete I’ll retain only replay-stable terms that improve both the fit and isolated-peak width audit.

The width comparison is unambiguous for CaWO₄: isotropic size broadening (about 0.087 µm) gives Rwp = 3.366%, substantially better than isotropic strain (4.667%). The common Lorentzian X term then became unnecessary; I fixed it at zero, removed the frozen/bound entry, reopened, and replayed with unchanged Rwp. WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ size/strain terms hit limits in their one-at-a-time trials, so I’m re-testing them only after this supported CaWO₄ term is fixed.

I’ve now isolated the dominant broadening to finite size in scheelite CaWO₄. I’m checking whether the much smaller Ca₃WO₆ width improvement is genuinely constrained or just another limit effect; then I’ll freeze the supported baseline and re-screen the weak-phase watch list against it.

The Ca₃WO₆ broadening is real and well determined: the size branch gives 0.255(12) µm and fits better than its strain alternative. The WO₃ terms remain either negligible or boundary-seeking. I’m retaining the two complementary size terms and doing one last WO₃ replay from that stabilized model, followed by the precursor/alternate-phase challenge.

The fair comparison confirms Ca₃WO₆ size broadening (Rwp 3.306%) over strain (3.315%); WO₃ strain is finite and replay-stable, while WO₃ size runs to 10 µm and is rejected. The retained model is therefore CaWO₄ size + WO₃ strain + Ca₃WO₆ size. I’m now adding each plausible precursor, hydrate/carbonate, alternate tungstate, and alternate WO₃ polymorph one at a time to ensure no weak phase survives.

The one-at-a-time phase challenges are still refining in parallel; they are CPU-bound rather than stalled. Each trial starts from the same saved three-phase model and is being reopened/replayed, so a rejected candidate cannot perturb the retained project.

The first watch-list pass exposed a useful modeling issue: several absent weak candidates drove their freely refined cells nonphysical instead of converging to zero. I’m tightening that test to the correct weak-phase protocol—source cell fixed, nonnegative scale only, small starting abundance, with all shared nuisance terms replayed. CaO is the only candidate that showed a potentially coherent trace signal in the loose pass, so it will receive an explicit peak-by-peak check after the restrained rerun.

The restrained watch-list result is clean at practical levels: carbonate and monoclinic CaWO₄ collapse to zero; orthorhombic WO₃ and the calcium–tungsten bronze are below 0.03 wt% with uncertainty at least as large. CaO and Ca(OH)₂ return only 0.11(5) and 0.32(10) wt%, respectively, and their main lines overlap the retained phases. I’ll treat those as unresolved trace upper-bound signals unless their non-overlapping reflections form a coherent residual family.

The deconvolved peak audit shows the retained shape model is behaving physically: CaWO₄ widths agree within about 0.01° at low/mid/high angle, Ca₃WO₆ agrees at 19.3°, 50.8°, and 74.9°, and WO₃ agrees at low/mid angle with only a weak high-angle discrepancy. There is no repeatable hkl-dependent width error to justify anisotropy. The remaining structured residual is chiefly relative intensity in the monoclinic WO₃ triplet, so I’m testing whether a restrained structural-coordinate refinement improves that family without destabilizing chemistry or fractions.

The coordinate test is physically acceptable and materially improves the fit: refining only the two W sites in monoclinic WO₃ lowers Rwp from 3.306% to 3.130%, and refining the single scheelite O site lowers it further to 3.105%. The resulting W–O distances remain in the expected distorted-octahedral range, and scheelite W–O is 1.776 Å. I’m accepting those limited coordinate refinements; Ca₃WO₆ coordinates remain fixed to the charge-balanced analogue because its ~10 wt% contribution does not support a free positional model. I’m now repeating the restrained weak-phase screen from this accepted structure.

The final watch-list replay leaves only Ca(OH)₂ as a nonzero candidate: 0.61(10) wt% with a small Rwp improvement. Because its strongest lines overlap CaWO₄/WO₃, that formal significance may come from scale redistribution rather than independent peaks. I’m running a stricter detection test with every accepted parameter frozen and only the candidate scale variable; I’ll retain the hydrate only if its non-overlapping lines improve coherently in that test.

The stricter scale-only test confirms a positive Ca(OH)₂ coefficient, but all of its useful intensity comes from lines coincident with the three retained phases (33.9°, 47.0°, 54.2°); its only relatively independent low-angle line is below 1σ. I’m therefore treating it as an unresolved trace/upper-bound signal, not a reliably quantified fourth phase. The three-phase model also survives the roughness challenge: removing roughness worsens Rwp from 3.105% to 3.190% while shifting modeled fractions by under 0.1 wt%. I’m freezing the three-phase result now and performing the required full-range save/reopen/replay and output audit.

The full 9.903–100.095° range has now been refined, saved, reopened, and replayed. The reopened project converged at Rwp 3.103% with all 8,301 points, and the generated profile, CIFs, and normalized fractions pass an exact cross-check against `final.gpx`. I’m assembling the concise evidence report now, including the nine measured peak-width checks and the trace-phase limitation.

Completed and validated against the reopened GSAS-II project.

Refined crystalline composition:

- CaWO₄ scheelite: 77.709 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 12.164 wt%
- Ca₃WO₆: 10.127 wt%

Fit: Rwp 3.103%, Rp 2.245%, Rexp 2.247%. A possible 0.45–0.61 wt% Ca(OH)₂ signal is too overlapped to identify reliably and was excluded.

